National Forum on Human Rights 2020. More information: https://nztf.lt/en/

With translation to/from English.

Moderator: Vytis Jurkonis, head of Freedom House in Lithuania

Discussion participants:

Dr. Anaïs Marin, UN Special Rapporteur on the situation of human rights in Belarus;
Dr. Dainius Žalimas, President of the Constitutional Court of Lithuania;
Dr. Wolfgang Benedek, OSCE Moscow Mechanism rapporteur (TBC), professor emeritus of University of Graz (Austria);
Enira Bronitskaya, lawyer at the Belarus human rights organisation Human Constanta;
Leslie Scanlon, Canadian Ambassador to Poland and Belarus.

Lithuania’s neighbour Belarus is the only European country where death penalty is not abolished. For this reason, it is not a member of the Council of Europe. People who disappear and restrictions of the freedom of speech and conscience are daily occurrences common to the current authoritarian regime of Belarus. In 2012, the United Nations established the position of special rapporteur on the situation of human rights in Belarus. This mandate is extended annually; since 2018, the position of this special rapporteur is occupied by Anaïs Marin (France), who has been prohibited from entering Belarus by its authorities.

The mass protests related to election forgery that followed the presidential election in Belarus on 9 August 2020 have been accompanied by imprisonment and torture. Those affected are counted by the thousands, there are casualties, hundreds have been injured or disappeared. How does the world react to human rights violations in Belarus?

The European Union has declared sanctions, the United Nations continue to monitor the status of human rights in Belarus. What else can we, the society and state of Lithuania, do to stop the human rights violations in Belarus? What legal and political processes could help ensure the protection of human rights in Belarus?
